**Question:**
How can I solve the following optimization problem using CVX?

\[
\begin{eqnarray*}
\text{minimize}_\textbf{x} &&  x_1^2 + 2x_1x_2 + 2x_2^2 - 3x_1 + x_2 \\
\text{subject to} && x_1 + x_2 = 1 \\
&& x_1 \geq 0 \\
&& x_2 \geq 0
\end{eqnarray*}
\]

**Answer:**
The objective function \( f(x_1, x_2) = x_1^2 + 2x_1x_2 + 2x_2^2 - 3x_1 + x_2 \) is convex because its Hessian matrix is positive definite:

\[
\text{Hessian} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 2 \\ 2 & 4 \end{bmatrix}
\]

The eigenvalues of this matrix are both positive, confirming convexity. The correct CVX code to solve this problem is:

```matlab
cvx_begin
    variable x(2,1);
    dual variables y1 y2 y3;
    minimize (1/2*quad_form(x,[2 2;2 4]) + 3*x(1) - x(2));
    subject to
        y1 : x(1) + x(2) == 1;
        y2 : x(1) >= 0;
        y3 : x(2) >= 0;
cvx_end
```

This code uses `quad_form` to handle the quadratic part of the objective function correctly. The linear part \( 3x(1) - x(2) \) is added separately. This formulation ensures that CVX recognizes the problem as convex and solves it accordingly.

